────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. rank([[1]], 2**32)
Rust raises ValueError in validate_p (p >= 2**32), while Python does 1 % 4294967296 and returns 1.

2. rank([[1]], -1)
Rust raises ValueError for negative p, while Python has 1 % -1 == 0 so the matrix is all-zero and returns 0.

3. force([[1, 0]], 1, [], -1)
Rust raises ValueError on the first % p, while Python reduces every entry to 0 mod -1, never calls pow, and returns (False, set()).

4. force([[1, 0]], 1, [], 2**32)
Rust raises ValueError for p >= 2**32, while Python runs the loop mod 2**32 and returns (False, set()).

5. force([], 1048577, range(1048577), 2147483647)
Rust raises ValueError because n > 2**20 before looking at T0, while Python sees len(set(T0)) == n and returns (True, set(range(1048577))) without entering the loop.

6. rank([[2**63]], 2147483647)
Rust/PyO3 raises OverflowError extracting the entry as i64, while Python reduces 2**63 % 2147483647 (== 2) and returns 1.

7. force([], 1, [2**63], 2147483647)
Rust raises OverflowError extracting T0, while Python does T=set([2**63]), skips the loop because len(T)==n, and returns (True, {2**63}).

8. rank([[0.0]], 2147483647)
Rust raises TypeError converting float to i64, while Python does 0.0 % p == 0.0, never calls _inv, and returns 0.

9. rank(iter([]), 2147483647)
Rust treats the iterator as truthy, extracts zero rows, and returns 0; Python does not rows is false, builds B=[], then hits IndexError on B[0].

10. rank([[1, "x"]], 0)
Rust TypeErrors while converting rows to i64 before looking at p; Python evaluates 1 % 0 first and raises ZeroDivisionError.

11. force([{0: 1, 1: 2147483646}], 1, [], 2147483647)
Python indexes the dict (r[0], r[1]) so the row is (1, p-1) and returns (True, {0}); Rust iterates dict keys so the row is (0, 1) and returns (False, set()).

12. force([[1]], 1, [], -5)
Rust sees 2*U[0] < len(row) and raises ValueError for negative p before reading column 1; Python computes 1 % -5 then raises IndexError on r[1].

13. rank([[0]], 2.5)
Rust TypeErrors extracting float p as i64; Python does 0 % 2.5 == 0.0 and returns 0 without calling three-arg pow.

14. force([], 1, "0", 2147483647)
Rust TypeErrors converting the character "0" to i64; Python does set("0") == {"0"}, len==n, and returns (True, {"0"}).

15. rank([[1]], 2**63)
Rust/PyO3 raises OverflowError converting p to i64 (2**63 is just outside signed 64-bit); Python runs mod 2**63 and returns 1.
